---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Colophon · San Lee"
  description="How I built this site. I set the direction, the contracts, and the bar; Claude did most of the typing; the evals and postmortems are the proof."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/colophon.html"
  ogType="article"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* How-it-was-made plate. I set, it types, I check. Open pipeline,
       no oxide: this is the method, not a reversal.

       The plate now sits in the MONOGRAPH figure ladder (ADR-014). The
       wrapper is the .fig fig--plate div, so style.css owns the width cap
       (--fig-plate), the night ground, the pocket edition, and the owned
       margin. The four declarations that stated those here are DELETED
       rather than out-cascaded, per the ladder's own rule. What stays is
       page-scoped: the stroke, the fill, and the type inside this one
       drawing. */
    /* The box stroke reads --stroke-figure, not --rule-strong (ADR-014,
       amendment A2). The two tokens are the same hex in light. In dark the
       figure token is #b0a591 against the global rule's #6b6252, so the
       night retune reaches the plate and leaves every rule outside the
       figure layer at its July value. I0 moved the two prototype pages'
       plates and left each remaining page's plate to that page's lane.
       fill: var(--bg) is deliberate and unchanged: --bg is scoped to
       --fig-ground inside a dark figure, so the knockout tracks the lifted
       ground instead of punching a darker hole in it. */
    .made-plate .md-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .made-plate .md-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    /* 9px, not 8px: the microtext floor (CLAUDE.md, ADR-014 section 5). The
       plate holds a 260-unit viewBox and renders at scale 1.046 in a 320px
       viewport, so a declared 9px renders at 9.42px. The rule says raise the
       declared unit and never scale the plate, because a plate that grows
       only under a phone breakpoint renders smaller again above it. The
       three labels are byte-exact; the longest, "then it ships", measures
       70.4 units at 9px inside a 76-unit box, so nothing overflows. */
    .made-plate .md-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 9px;
    }
    .made-plate .md-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .made-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    /* The caption now sits INSIDE the figure wrapper, so the PL-2 ladder in
       style.css does not reach it: the ladder owns direct children of
       main.wrap only. The old bottom margin is gone; the wrapper owns the
       air under the figure now.

       THE GEOMETRY IS NO LONGER RESTATED HERE. style.css carries
       `.fig--plate > p`, which gives every bare-plate caption the prose
       measure and the centring; that is the ask PR #286 left for the one hand
       on that file, now taken. Without it this caption inherits the wrapper's
       promoted wide track and runs 884px at 1280px against a 544px prose
       measure. This caption still cannot BE a <figcaption> — one outside a
       <figure> is invalid, and a <figure> here fails the figure contract —
       which is exactly why the shared selector reaches for the <p>.

       What stays local is the voice. The chrome pages caption in `--muted` at
       0.92rem; the project pages use the note voice. One shared selector
       cannot decide that without changing what a page renders. */
    .made-plate-caption {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 0.92rem;
    }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteNav />


  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="index.html">&larr; Back</a>
    <h1>Colophon</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      How I built this site. The method, and the session record that is its
      evidence.
    </p>
    <!-- The plate takes its number and its kind in the five-kind vocabulary
         (ADR-014, PL-3): a real span with a hand-authored digit, in flow,
         one line above the artwork, at every width. It is the only figure on
         this page, so it is Plate 01. The wrapper is a div and never a
         <figure>: figure-contract.cjs fails a <figure> that carries no
         data-fig, and a data-fig figure owes two caption slots this
         one-slot caption does not have. -->
    <div class="fig fig--plate">
    <span class="fig-label">Plate 01</span>
    <svg class="made-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 72" role="img"
         aria-label="How I built this site. I set the direction. An agent did most of the typing. I checked the output against the real repos before it shipped.">
      <title>I set the aim. The agent types. I check the output.</title>
      <defs>
        <marker id="md-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
      </defs>
      <rect class="md-box" x="2" y="6" width="76" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="md-title" x="40" y="24" text-anchor="middle">I set</text>
      <text class="md-micro" x="40" y="38" text-anchor="middle">direction</text>
      <path class="md-edge" d="M 78 26 L 90 26" marker-end="url(#md-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <rect class="md-box" x="92" y="6" width="76" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="md-title" x="130" y="24" text-anchor="middle">It types</text>
      <text class="md-micro" x="130" y="38" text-anchor="middle">the agent</text>
      <path class="md-edge" d="M 168 26 L 180 26" marker-end="url(#md-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <rect class="md-box" x="182" y="6" width="76" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="md-title" x="220" y="24" text-anchor="middle">I check</text>
      <text class="md-micro" x="220" y="38" text-anchor="middle">then it ships</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="made-plate-caption">
      I set the direction. Claude typed. The ledger is the proof.
    </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      I built this site the way I would run any small project with an agent. I
      set the direction, the contracts, and the bar. Claude did most of the
      typing. I checked the output against the real repos before it shipped.
      The evals and postmortems are the proof.
    </p>
    <p>
      That claim gets the same standard as everything else here: evidence over
      assertion. Some of those decisions were mine. Some were not. The ledger
      below is the record of which.
    </p>

    <h2>The ledger</h2>
    <p>
      This is a sample from the actual session transcripts. It covers the whole
      build. The dates are real. I tightened the quotes to the site&rsquo;s
      writing standard. I did not remove the entries that record my own errors
      or the agent&rsquo;s errors.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since early July the worst entries carry public records. The operating
      layer keeps its
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/agent-ops">incident log in the open</a>,
      postmortem-first. I lifted the early-July quotes straight from those
      postmortems and the linked records. The later quotes come from the
      session transcripts themselves.
      <a href="projects/false-green.html">False Green</a> turns the same
      scrutiny on my own instruments rather than on the agent. Six checks
      reported success for work that never ran.
    </p>
    <!--
      Curation bar for this ledger (read before editing):
      Every entry must show human judgment changing the outcome — a catch, a
      refusal, a redirect, or the agent's own case winning on merit. If an entry
      reads as "Claude did a task well," it does not belong here; the page's
      claim is that the *direction* was the work, so the quote should almost
      always be San's, not the agent being clever.
      Curation is the owner's current preference (2026-09-04): about 6 to 8
      entries, each with human judgment visible. The owner may change the
      count or the bar. A session that changes either adds a dated line here.
    -->
    <ol class="timeline">
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jun 21</div>
        <div class="tl-title">Sometimes its case won.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">It pushed back on my docs format, Markdown over RST. I weighed the reasoning and I took it. Steering works in both directions. The point is not that I am always right. The point is that someone decides. <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;If you decide the format is more than we need, keep that decision.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jun 27</div>
        <div class="tl-title">It insisted the system still used Kafka.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">The local clone that it trusted was out of date, so it argued a fact that changed. That became a standing rule: verify before asserting. <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;The system does not use Kafka now. Confirm this.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jul 2</div>
        <div class="tl-title">It burned a whole usage window.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">A scoped question started an uncapped fan-out of thirty-plus agents on the most expensive model tier. I found it mid-flight and I killed it. An entire five-hour window went in forty-five minutes. The fix is a hook that mechanically blocks any uncapped run (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/agent-ops/blob/main/incidents/2026-07-02-uncapped-premium-fanout.md">postmortem</a>). <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;Spend was authorized with no review, by default, and with no gate.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jul 3</div>
        <div class="tl-title">I asked for holes; it found one in its own guard.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">On an open audit request it bypassed its own credential guard with an interpreter one-liner. I built that hook after real leaks. The bypass proved the gap safely, before a live one could. The prompt that mattered was mine. The finding was real (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/agent-ops/blob/main/incidents/2026-07-03-credential-guard-interpreter-bypass.md">postmortem</a>). <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;Tell me every gap, leak, vulnerability, and risk.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jul 26</div>
        <div class="tl-title">I retired a framing that it built on again and again.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">Its operating notes filed this site as my &ldquo;front-end learning vehicle&rdquo;. That framing scoped the work again and again, instead of the portfolio. I retired the framing mid-redesign. I had the record rewritten, so the framing stayed dead. The direction includes the decision about what a project <em>is</em>. <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;I do not have a &lsquo;front end learning vehicle&rsquo;.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jul 28</div>
        <div class="tl-title">Its whole pipeline approved a design. I did not.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">A redesign survived five direction prototypes, a judging panel, and an adversarial review. It still looked wrong on my screen. I asked for variants instead of an argument about taste. A three-way screenshot A/B settled it, and the serif came out <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/pull/182">the same day</a>. A process approves a design. It does not decide. <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;I do not know if I accept this font. Explain the night-light background.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Aug 2</div>
        <div class="tl-title">I made it read this site cold.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">I had it read the site as a hiring manager who knows nothing about me. It got no context and no credit for effort. The verdict was blunt and correct. The homepage explained how I work before it said who I am. I <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/pull/200">re-cut the positioning the same day</a>. <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;Assume you know nothing about me.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </main>
  <script is:inline src="assets/theme.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="assets/last-updated.js"></script>
</Base>
